SCEN = [
 ('w03', 'B1 · Warehouse stereo AMR', r'''An autonomous mobile robot carries a rectified stereo camera (f = 800 px, disparity noise σ_d ≈ 0.5 px, disparity search range 128 px). It must see pallets at 5 m with depth error ≤ 0.1 m, and it runs stereo VO with P3P + RANSAC.
<ol><li>What minimum baseline meets the 5 m requirement? (3)</li><li>With that baseline, what is the closest measurable depth? What does this mean for docking at 0.5 m? (3)</li><li>About 60% of stereo matches are inliers. How many RANSAC iterations give 99.9% confidence with P3P? (2)</li><li>Name two pipeline stages that reduce drift beyond frame-to-frame PnP. (2)</li></ol>''',
 r'''<ol><li>\(\delta Z \approx Z^2\delta d/(fb) \le 0.1\) → \(b \ge Z^2\delta d/(f\cdot0.1) = 25\cdot0.5/80 =\) <b>0.156 m</b>. (With b = 0.2 m, δZ = 0.078 m.)</li>
<li>\(Z_{min} = fb/d_{max} = 800\cdot0.156/128 =\) <b>0.98 m</b>. At 0.5 m the disparity would be 250 px, outside the 128 px search, so the robot is blind for docking. Fixes: a larger search range, a second short-baseline or depth sensor, or a docking camera.</li>
<li>\(k = \log(0.001)/\log(1-0.6^3) = 28.4\) → <b>29 iterations</b>.</li>
<li>Keyframe-based estimation (relative to the last keyframe, not every frame); a pose graph with non-consecutive and loop-closure edges; windowed BA over landmarks; fusing an IMU or wheel odometry. Any two, with a reason.</li></ol>'''),
 ('w13', 'B2 · Pallet and person detector in a busy warehouse', r'''A detector flags <i>person</i> and <i>pallet</i> for a forklift robot. Validation shows: a reliability bin at confidence 0.9 with 78% accuracy; 6 wrong out of 170 accepted predictions when the robot abstains on 30 of 200 frames; and a new kind of motorised pallet jack (never in training) detected as “pallet” with 0.95 confidence.
<ol><li>Is the model over- or under-confident in that bin, and what cheap fix would you apply? Where do you fit it? (3)</li><li>Compute coverage and selective risk. (2)</li><li>Classify the pallet-jack issue (shift type) and explain why confidence did not warn you. Suggest a signal that might. (3)</li><li>For the <i>person</i> class, which error type matters more and how would you set the threshold? (2)</li></ol>''',
 r'''<ol><li>Accuracy 0.78 &lt; confidence 0.90 → <b>over-confident</b> by 0.12. Apply temperature scaling (T &gt; 1), fitted on held-out <b>validation</b> data (never the test set); it doesn’t change predicted classes and may not hold under shift.</li>
<li>Coverage = 170/200 = <b>0.85</b>; selective risk = 6/170 = <b>0.035</b>.</li>
<li>Novel class / open-set input (\(p_{train}(x,y^*)=0\)). Softmax must spread probability over known classes and the score grows with distance from the decision boundary, so a novel object can get high confidence. Better signals: feature-space distance to training data, energy score, ensemble / MC-dropout disagreement (epistemic uncertainty), and open-vocabulary prompts for the new class.</li>
<li>False negatives (a missed person) are far more costly than false stops, so favour <b>recall</b>: lower the person threshold, chosen on validation data for a target recall, and accept more unnecessary stops. Slow down when uncertain.</li></ol>'''),
 ('w07', 'B3 · Drone mapping a 1 km loop around a mine site', r'''A drone runs monocular VO with an IMU. At the end of a 1 km loop it is 14 m from its start position, although it physically returned to the start. Aligned position errors at four checkpoints are 0.1, 0.2, 0.2 and 0.4 m during the first 100 m.
<ol><li>Compute the ATE RMSE for the four checkpoints. Which metric would better show the per-step drift rate? (3)</li><li>Explain how SLAM would correct the 14 m error, naming the perception step and the optimisation step. (4)</li><li>Why fuse the IMU at all, if vision is available? Give two reasons. (3)</li></ol>''',
 r'''<ol><li>RMSE = √((0.01+0.04+0.04+0.16)/4) = √0.0625 = <b>0.25 m</b>. RPE (relative pose error) shows local drift per step; ATE mixes early errors into every later position.</li>
<li><b>Place recognition</b> (VPR: global-descriptor retrieval, e.g. bag of words or NetVLAD/AnyLoc) detects the return to the start; <b>geometric verification</b> (epipolar/PnP RANSAC) confirms it and gives the relative transform; that becomes a <b>loop-closure factor</b> in a <b>pose graph</b>, and nonlinear least squares (LM) spreads the 14 m correction along the trajectory weighted by the edge covariances; optionally global BA afterwards (as in ORB-SLAM3).</li>
<li>The IMU gives metric scale for monocular vision (gravity and acceleration in m/s²); it works through motion blur, dust, darkness and low texture; and at 1–10 kHz it predicts feature motion between frames. Vision bounds the IMU’s quadratic bias drift in return.</li></ol>'''),
]

PAPER = [
 ('w02', 'C1 · Research excerpt: ablation of a learned feature matcher (SuperGlue, CVPR 2020, Table 4, real data)', r'''Indoor pose estimation with SuperPoint features. Columns: pose AUC@20° (%), match precision (%), matching score (%).
<div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Matcher</th><th>AUC@20°</th><th>Precision</th><th>Matching score</th></tr>
<tr><td>NN + mutual check</td><td>36.40</td><td>50.4</td><td>18.8</td></tr><tr><td>No graph neural net</td><td>38.56</td><td>66.0</td><td>17.2</td></tr><tr><td>No cross-attention</td><td>42.57</td><td>74.0</td><td>25.3</td></tr><tr><td>No positional encoding</td><td>47.12</td><td>75.8</td><td>26.6</td></tr><tr><td>Smaller (3 layers)</td><td>46.93</td><td>79.9</td><td>30.0</td></tr><tr><td>Full (9 layers)</td><td>51.84</td><td>84.4</td><td>31.5</td></tr></table></div>
<ol><li>What does “NN + mutual check” correspond to in the lecture’s classical pipeline? (1)</li><li>Which single component removal hurts pose accuracy most, and what does that component do? (3)</li><li>Precision rises from 50.4 to 84.4%. Why does that matter for the RANSAC stage that follows? Use the iteration formula. (3)</li><li>Give one limitation this table cannot reveal. (1)</li></ol>''',
 r'''<ol><li>Brute-force nearest-neighbour descriptor matching with the cross-check (mutual nearest neighbour) filter from W2.</li>
<li>Removing the <b>graph neural network</b> (attention-based context aggregation): AUC 51.84 → 38.56 (−13.3), barely above the NN baseline. Of the ablations that keep the GNN, removing <b>cross-attention</b> hurts most (42.57): cross-attention lets features exchange information between the two images to find consistent candidates.</li>
<li>Higher precision = higher inlier ratio w. \(k = \log(1-p)/\log(1-w^n)\): with n = 5 (E) and p = 0.99, w = 0.50 needs 146 iterations, while w = 0.84 needs only 9 (8.5 rounded up). Fewer iterations and more reliable, better-spread inliers give better pose estimates.</li>
<li>Runtime and memory cost (69 ms on a GPU), generalisation to other domains, dependence on detector quality, behaviour in low texture (sparse method).</li></ol>'''),
 ('w13', 'C2 · Research excerpt: uncertainty vs training data (Kendall & Gal, NeurIPS 2017, Table 3b, real data)', r'''Semantic segmentation models trained on subsets of CamVid (street scenes) and tested on CamVid or NYUv2 (indoor). Aleatoric = mean entropy; epistemic = mean logit variance (×10⁻³).
<div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Train</th><th>Test</th><th>IoU</th><th>Aleatoric</th><th>Epistemic</th></tr>
<tr><td>CamVid/4</td><td>CamVid</td><td>57.2</td><td>0.106</td><td>1.96</td></tr><tr><td>CamVid/2</td><td>CamVid</td><td>62.9</td><td>0.156</td><td>1.66</td></tr><tr><td>CamVid</td><td>CamVid</td><td>67.5</td><td>0.111</td><td>1.36</td></tr><tr><td>CamVid/4</td><td>NYUv2</td><td>–</td><td>0.247</td><td>10.9</td></tr><tr><td>CamVid</td><td>NYUv2</td><td>–</td><td>0.264</td><td>11.8</td></tr></table></div>
<ol><li>How does each uncertainty type change with training-set size? Explain using the lecture definitions. (3)</li><li>What happens on NYUv2, and what kind of distribution shift is it? (2)</li><li>A street robot will run on a small embedded GPU in real time. Which uncertainty would you model and why? What do you give up? (3)</li></ol>''',
 r'''<ol><li>Epistemic falls steadily (1.96 → 1.66 → 1.36) as data grows, because it is lack of knowledge, reducible with data. Aleatoric shows no trend (0.106, 0.156, 0.111) because it is noise inherent in the observations, not reducible with data.</li>
<li>Epistemic rises about 5–9× (to 10.9–11.8): the model sees inputs unlike its training data. This is OOD covariate shift (street → indoor); some indoor classes are effectively novel. Aleatoric also roughly doubles here, a nuance against the paper’s claim that aleatoric doesn’t increase for out-of-data inputs.</li>
<li>Aleatoric: nearly free at inference (one pass), and good for large-data regimes. You give up epistemic uncertainty, i.e. detecting novel situations. MC dropout costs ~50× for 50 samples. For safety, consider a cheaper OOD signal (energy score, feature distance) instead.</li></ol>'''),
 ('w12', 'C3 · Research excerpt (synthetic, for practice): place recognition across conditions', r'''<p class="note"><b>Synthetic table</b>, written for practice. It is not from a real paper.</p>Recall@1 / Recall@5 (%) for three VPR descriptors.
<div class="tw"><table><tr><th>Method</th><th>Urban day→day</th><th>Urban day→night</th><th>Underwater</th><th>Descriptor size</th><th>Query time</th></tr>
<tr><td>A: NetVLAD (trained on urban)</td><td>86 / 94</td><td>41 / 58</td><td>9 / 21</td><td>4096</td><td>8 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>B: foundation features + VLAD (no training)</td><td>84 / 93</td><td>63 / 79</td><td>48 / 70</td><td>49152</td><td>95 ms</td></tr>
<tr><td>C: B + geometric re-ranking of top-10</td><td>89 / 94</td><td>70 / 80</td><td>53 / 70</td><td>49152 + local</td><td>310 ms</td></tr></table></div>
<ol><li>What does the drop of A from day→day to underwater suggest? (2)</li><li>Why do C’s Recall@5 values barely differ from B’s while Recall@1 improves? (2)</li><li>For loop closure in a SLAM system running at 10 Hz, which method would you choose and how would you deploy it? (3)</li></ol>''',
 r'''<ol><li>A is environment-specific: trained on urban data, it generalises poorly to a new domain (a distribution shift in appearance). Recall@1 falls from 86% to 9%, the AnyLoc motivation.</li>
<li>Re-ranking only reorders the top-10 candidates. If the correct image is already in the top 5, R@5 stays the same, but geometry pushes it to rank 1, so R@1 rises. Re-ranking can’t recover matches missing from the candidate list.</li>
<li>For example, B or C at keyframe rate (not every frame): global descriptors for fast candidate search, then geometric verification (C) only on the top-K to avoid false loops (precision first). 310 ms is too slow per frame but fine per keyframe in a separate thread, as in ORB-SLAM3. Reduce the descriptor size (PCA) if memory is tight. A, if the robot only ever drives in daytime urban areas.</li></ol>'''),
]

def render(mcq, openq):
    parts = ['''<section data-panel="mock" hidden aria-label="Mock exam"><div><p class="note mono">New paper · same format as the 2026 practice exam</p><h2>Mock exam</h2>
<p class="lead">Section A mirrors the practice exam: 40 single-answer MCQs with the same topic spread. Sections B and C follow the two extra question types described in <code>exam-structure.txt</code>; their format and marks are <b>my assumption</b>, because the real layout isn’t published. Time yourself, answer on paper, then open the solutions and self-mark.</p></div>
<div class="sub"><h3>Section A · Multiple choice (40 × 1 mark, assumed)</h3>''']
    for i, (t, stem, opts, ans, why) in enumerate(MCQ):
        parts.append(mcq(f'{t}-mock{i+1}', stem, opts, ans, why, (t.upper().replace('W0', 'W'), 'MCQ'), num=f'A{i+1}'))
    parts.append('</div><div class="sub"><h3>Section B · Scenario questions (10 marks each, assumed)</h3>')
    for i, (t, title, q, a) in enumerate(SCEN):
        parts.append(openq(f'{t}-mockB{i+1}', f'<b>{title}</b><div>{q}</div>', a, ('scenario', '10 marks'), num=f'B{i+1}'))
    parts.append('</div><div class="sub"><h3>Section C · Research-paper excerpts (8 marks each, assumed)</h3>')
    for i, (t, title, q, a) in enumerate(PAPER):
        parts.append(openq(f'{t}-mockC{i+1}', f'<b>{title}</b><div>{q}</div>', a, ('paper excerpt', '7–8 marks'), num=f'C{i+1}'))
    parts.append('</div></section>')
    return '\n'.join(parts)
